{"source": "S2", "quote": "exceeds thirty days, the contractor shall, in addition, be entitled to such compensation as the Engineer-in- Charge may consider reasonable"},
{"source": "S2", "quote": "adding thereto 2% to cover indirect expenses of the contractor provided the contractor submits his claim supported by details to the Engineer-in- Charge within fifteen days of the expiry of the period of 30 days."}
],
"answer": "Safety of the works is one of the permitted reasons for suspension, listed as reason (c), and it is separate from contractor default, which is reason (a) [S1]. For suspension under (b) or (c), you get an extension of time equal to the period of the suspension plus 25%. The extension applies to the item or group of items with a separate completion period that the suspended work belongs to [S1]. Idle labour can be recovered, but only if the total of all such suspensions for that item or group exceeds thirty days. In that case you are entitled to compensation the Engineer-in-Charge considers reasonable for salaries and wages paid to your employees and labour idle at site, plus 2% for indirect expenses [S2]. You must submit the claim with supporting details within fifteen days of the expiry of the 30-day period [S2]. The amount is therefore at the Engineer-in-Charge's discretion, and the sources give no compensation for idle labour while total suspensions stay within thirty days [S2].",
"missing": "The sources do not state how the 'reasonable' compensation is calculated beyond the 2% addition. They also do not say how the extension is applied if several items are affected."}

